%% chapter-11.ltx — Proof Theory and the Curry--Howard Correspondence
%%

The Curry--Howard correspondence---also known as the ``propositions-as-types'' principle---is one of the deepest insights in computer science. It reveals that proofs and programs are fundamentally the same kind of object: a proof of a proposition is a program that computes a value of the corresponding type. This insight, which emerged independently from the work of Curry, Howard, de Bruijn, and Martin-Löf, has transformed both proof theory and programming language theory.

This chapter develops the Curry--Howard correspondence in full generality: from propositional proofs as programs, through predicate proofs as dependent programs, to sequent calculus and cut elimination. We explore the implications for compiler verification and program correctness.

\section{Propositional Proofs as Programs}\label{sec:ch-prop}

The Curry--Howard correspondence for propositional logic was first observed by Curry (1934) for the implicational fragment and by Howard (1970) for full intuitionistic logic.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Curry--Howard correspondence: propositional logic and STLC}
\label{tab:ch-prop}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Logic} & \textbf{Type Theory} & \textbf{Programming} \\
\hline
Proposition $A$ & Type $A$ & Data type \\
Proof $p : A$ & Term $t : A$ & Program \\
$A \to B$ (implication) & $A \to B$ (function type) & Function type \\
$A \land B$ (conjunction) & $A \times B$ (product type) & Pair type \\
$A \lor B$ (disjunction) & $A + B$ (sum type) & Tagged union \\
$\top$ (truth) & $\mathbf{1}$ (unit type) & Unit \\
$\bot$ (falsity) & $\mathbf{0}$ (empty type) & Empty type \\
$\Rightarrow$-introduction & $\lambda$-abstraction & Function definition \\
$\Rightarrow$-elimination (modus ponens) & Application & Function call \\
$\land$-introduction & Pairing & Tuple construction \\
$\land$-elimination & Projection & Field access \\
$\lor$-introduction & Injection & Variant construction \\
$\lor$-elimination & Case analysis & Pattern matching \\
$\bot$-elimination & Abort & \texttt{absurd} / \texttt{impossible} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{theorem}[Curry--Howard for Propositional Logic]\label{thm:ch-prop}
A proposition $\varphi$ is provable in intuitionistic propositional logic if and only if the corresponding type $\tau_\varphi$ is inhabited in the STLC (with products and sums).
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[Modus Ponens as Application]\label{ex:ch-mp}
The logical rule of modus ponens:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{A \quad A \to B}{B}$
\end{center}
corresponds to function application:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{f : A \to B \quad a : A}{f\;a : B}$
\end{center}
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Conjunction Introduction as Pairing]\label{ex:ch-and-intro}
The logical rule $\land$-introduction:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{A \quad B}{A \land B}$
\end{center}
corresponds to pair construction:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{a : A \quad b : B}{(a, b) : A \times B}$
\end{center}
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Disjunction Elimination as Case Analysis]\label{ex:ch-or-elim}
The logical rule $\lor$-elimination:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{A \lor B \quad A \vdash C \quad B \vdash C}{C}$
\end{center}
corresponds to case analysis:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{e : A + B \quad f : A \to C \quad g : B \to C}{\mathrm{case}\;e\;\mathrm{of}\;\mathrm{inl}(x) \Rightarrow f(x)\;\mid\;\mathrm{inr}(y) \Rightarrow g(y) : C}$
\end{center}
\end{example}

\section{Predicate Proofs as Dependent Programs}\label{sec:ch-predicate}

The correspondence extends to first-order logic via dependent types.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Curry--Howard correspondence: predicate logic and dependent types}
\label{tab:ch-pred}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Predicate Logic} & \textbf{Dependent Type Theory} \\
\hline
Predicate $P(x)$ & Type family $P : A \to \mathcal{U}$ \\
$\forall x.\,P(x)$ & $\depFunTy{x}{A}{P(x)}$ ($\Pi$-type) \\
$\exists x.\,P(x)$ & $\depSigTy{x}{A}{P(x)}$ ($\Sigma$-type) \\
Equality $a =_A b$ & $\mathrm{Id}_A(a, b)$ (identity type) \\
Universal instantiation & Function application \\
Existential introduction & Pair construction \\
Equality substitution & $J$ rule (path induction) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{example}[Universal Quantification as Dependent Function]\label{ex:ch-forall}
A proof of $\forall n:\mathbb{N}.\,P(n)$ is a function that takes any $n : \mathbb{N}$ and returns a proof of $P(n)$:
\[\lambda n.\,\mathrm{proof}(n) : \depFunTy{n}{\mathbb{N}}{P(n)}\]
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Existential Quantification as Dependent Pair]\label{ex:ch-exists}
A proof of $\exists n:\mathbb{N}.\,P(n)$ is a pair consisting of a witness and a proof:
\[(42, \mathrm{proof}) : \depSigTy{n}{\mathbb{N}}{P(n)}\]
The witness $42$ is \emph{extracted} from the proof---this is the \emph{program extraction} capability of constructive type theory.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Equality as Identity Type]\label{ex:ch-equality}
A proof of $a =_A b$ is a term of type $\mathrm{Id}_A(a, b)$. The only canonical inhabitant is $\mathrm{refl}(a) : \mathrm{Id}_A(a, a)$, which proves that $a$ is equal to itself. The $J$ rule allows transporting proofs along equalities:
\[\mathrm{transport} : \depFunTy{P}{A \to \mathcal{U}}\depFunTy{a}{A}\depFunTy{b}{A}{\mathrm{Id}_A(a,b) \to P(a) \to P(b)}\]
\end{example}

\section{Sequent Calculus and Cut Elimination}\label{sec:ch-sequent}

Gentzen's sequent calculus provides a proof system that is particularly well-suited to the Curry--Howard correspondence.

\subsection{The Sequent Calculus $\mathbf{LJ}$}

\begin{definition}[Intuitionistic Sequent Calculus]\label{def:lj}
The sequent calculus $\mathbf{LJ}$ for intuitionistic propositional logic has sequents of the form $\Gamma \Rightarrow A$ (at most one formula on the right). The rules are:

\emph{Axiom}: $\Gamma, A \Rightarrow A$

\emph{Structural rules}:
\begin{center}
Weakening: $\dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow B}{\Gamma, A \Rightarrow B}$ \quad Contraction: $\dfrac{\Gamma, A, A \Rightarrow B}{\Gamma, A \Rightarrow B}$
\end{center}

\emph{Logical rules}:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma, A, B \Rightarrow C}{\Gamma, A \land B \Rightarrow C}\textsc{($\land$L)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow A \quad \Gamma \Rightarrow B}{\Gamma \Rightarrow A \land B}\textsc{($\land$R)}$

$\dfrac{\Gamma, A \Rightarrow C \quad \Gamma, B \Rightarrow C}{\Gamma, A \lor B \Rightarrow C}\textsc{($\lor$L)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow A}{\Gamma \Rightarrow A \lor B}\textsc{($\lor$R$_1$)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow B}{\Gamma \Rightarrow A \lor B}\textsc{($\lor$R$_2$)}$

$\dfrac{\Gamma, A \Rightarrow B}{\Gamma \Rightarrow A \to B}\textsc{($\to$R)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow A \to B \quad \Gamma \Rightarrow A}{\Gamma \Rightarrow B}\textsc{($\to$L)}$
\end{center}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Cut Elimination}

\begin{definition}[Cut Rule]\label{def:cut}
The \emph{cut} rule is:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow A \quad \Gamma, A \Rightarrow B}{\Gamma \Rightarrow B}\textsc{(Cut)}$
\end{center}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cut Elimination for $\mathbf{LJ}$]\label{thm:cut-elim-lj}
Every proof in $\mathbf{LJ}$ can be transformed into a cut-free proof.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By a procedure of \emph{cut elimination} (Gentzen's \emph{Hauptsatz}). The key observation is that a cut can always be ``pushed up'' the proof tree and eliminated by restructuring the proof. The procedure terminates because each step reduces the complexity of the cut formula.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Subformula Property]\label{def:subformula}
A proof system has the \emph{subformula property} if every formula occurring in a proof is a subformula of the conclusion.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Subformula Property for Cut-Free Proofs]\label{thm:subformula}
Cut-free proofs in $\mathbf{LJ}$ have the subformula property.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By inspection of the rules: every formula in a rule's premises is either a subformula of the conclusion or is the principal formula being introduced/eliminated. Without cut, no new formulas are introduced.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Cut Elimination as Program Simplification}

Under the Curry--Howard correspondence, the cut rule corresponds to \emph{let-binding} (or function application followed by substitution):
\[\dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow a : A \quad \Gamma, x:A \Rightarrow e : B}{\Gamma \Rightarrow \mathrm{let}\;x = a\;\mathrm{in}\;e : B}\]

Cut elimination corresponds to \emph{beta-reduction}: substituting the value $a$ for $x$ in $e$.

\begin{insight}
Cut elimination is program simplification. The cut rule introduces an intermediate value (a ``let-binding''); eliminating the cut substitutes the value directly. This is exactly what beta-reduction does: $(\lambda x.\,e)\;a \longrightarrow \subst{e}{x}{a}$. The normalization of proofs (cut elimination) corresponds to the normalization of programs (beta-reduction).
\end{insight}

\section{Natural Deduction and Normalization}\label{sec:ch-natded}

\subsection{Natural Deduction}

Natural deduction is the proof system most directly connected to programming. Each introduction rule corresponds to a constructor, and each elimination rule corresponds to a destructor.

\begin{definition}[Normal and Neutral Terms]\label{def:normal-neutral}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A term is \emph{normal} if it contains no beta-redexes.
  \item A term is \emph{neutral} if it is a variable or an application of a neutral term to a normal term.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Normal Proofs]\label{def:normal-proof}
A proof in natural deduction is \emph{normal} if it contains no \emph{detours}: no introduction rule immediately followed by the corresponding elimination rule.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Normalization of Natural Deduction]\label{thm:norm-natded}
Every proof in natural deduction can be normalized: transformed into a normal proof.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By iteratively eliminating detours (beta-reductions). Since the STLC is strongly normalizing (Theorem~\ref{thm:stlc-strong-norm}), this process terminates.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Normal Form of Proofs]\label{def:nf-proof}
A normal proof of $A \to B$ has the form: assume $A$, derive $B$ (introduction last). A normal proof of $A \land B$ has the form: derive $A$, derive $B$, pair them. A normal proof of $A \lor B$ has the form: derive $A$ (or $B$), inject.
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
The normal form of a proof corresponds to the \emph{canonical form} of a program. A normal proof of a conjunction ends with a pair constructor; a normal proof of an implication ends with a lambda abstraction. This is the computational content of proof normalization: normal proofs are ``values'' (fully evaluated programs).
\end{insight}

\section{Proof Irrelevance and Proof Relevance}\label{sec:ch-proof-irrelevance}

\begin{definition}[Proof Irrelevance]\label{def:proof-irrelevance}
A type theory is \emph{proof-irrelevant} if any two proofs of the same proposition are considered equal. In proof-irrelevant systems, the identity of a proof does not matter---only the fact that a proof exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proof Relevance]\label{def:proof-relevance}
A type theory is \emph{proof-relevant} if different proofs of the same proposition may be distinguishable. In proof-relevant systems, the identity of a proof matters---different proofs may have different computational content.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Proof irrelevance vs.\ proof relevance}
\label{tab:proof-irrelevance}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Proof-Irrelevant} & \textbf{Proof-Relevant} \\
\hline
Examples & Coq (Prop), HoTT (mere props) & Agda, Coq (Type), Lean \\
Equality of proofs & All proofs are equal & Proofs may differ \\
Extraction & No computational content & Programs can be extracted \\
Classical logic & Compatible & Problematic \\
Homotopy interpretation & Sets (0-types) & Spaces (higher types) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{insight}
The distinction between proof irrelevance and proof relevance is fundamental. In a proof-irrelevant system, a proof of $\exists x.\,P(x)$ merely asserts that such an $x$ exists---you cannot extract it. In a proof-relevant system, a proof of $\exists x.\,P(x)$ \emph{contains} the witness $x$, and you can extract it. This is the difference between classical and constructive mathematics.
\end{insight}

\section{Applications to Compiler Verification}\label{sec:ch-compiler-verification}

The Curry--Howard correspondence enables \emph{verified compilation}: compiling programs with a machine-checked proof that the compilation preserves semantics.

\subsection{CompCert}

CompCert~\cite{leroy2009compcert} is a verified C compiler developed by Xavier Leroy and colleagues. It is written in Coq, and its correctness theorem states:

\begin{theorem}[CompCert Correctness]\label{thm:compcert}
For any source program $S$ and compiled program $T$, if $\mathrm{compile}(S) = T$, then the observable behavior of $T$ is a safe approximation of the observable behavior of $S$.
\end{theorem}

This theorem is proved in Coq, using the Curry--Howard correspondence: the proof is a program, and the compiler is a certified program.

\subsection{Translation Validation}

An alternative to full compiler verification is \emph{translation validation}: checking that each individual compilation step preserves semantics. This can be formalized using the Curry--Howard correspondence:

\begin{definition}[Translation Validation]\label{def:translation-validation}
Given a compiler pass $f : \mathrm{IR}_1 \to \mathrm{IR}_2$, translation validation checks that for each input $p$, the output $f(p)$ is semantically equivalent to $p$. This is a proof of:
\[\forall p.\,\sembrack{p}_{\mathrm{IR}_1} = \sembrack{f(p)}_{\mathrm{IR}_2}\]
\end{definition}

\section{Classical Logic and Continuations}\label{sec:ch-classical}

The Curry--Howard correspondence for intuitionistic logic is well understood. What about classical logic?

\begin{definition}[Classical Axioms]\label{def:classical-axioms}
The following principles are valid in classical logic but not in intuitionistic logic:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Law of excluded middle}: $A \lor \neg A$
  \item \textbf{Double negation elimination}: $\neg\neg A \to A$
  \item \textbf{Peirce's law}: $((A \to B) \to A) \to A$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Continuations and Classical Logic]\label{def:continuations-classical}
Griffin (1990) and Felleisen (1990) observed that \emph{continuations} correspond to classical logic. The control operator $C$ (call-with-current-continuation) has type:
\[C : ((A \to \bot) \to \bot) \to A\]
which is exactly double negation elimination.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Classical Logic and Control Operators]\label{thm:classical-control}
Adding a control operator (continuation) to the simply-typed lambda calculus corresponds to adding the law of excluded middle (or double negation elimination) to intuitionistic logic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{insight}
This is a remarkable correspondence: the computational content of classical reasoning is \emph{control flow}. A proof by contradiction (assuming $\neg A$ and deriving a contradiction) corresponds to capturing the current continuation and aborting. This insight has led to the development of programming languages with classical reasoning built in.
\end{insight}

\section{Linear Logic and Resource Awareness}\label{sec:ch-linear}

\begin{definition}[Linear Logic]\label{def:linear-logic}
\emph{Linear logic} (Girard, 1987) is a logic in which every assumption must be used exactly once. It distinguishes between:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Linear} connectives: $\otimes$ (tensor), $\parr$ (par), $\multimap$ (linear implication)
  \item \emph{Exponential} connectives: $!A$ (``of course''), $?A$ (``why not'')
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Curry--Howard for linear logic}
\label{tab:ch-linear}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Linear Logic} & \textbf{Linear Types} & \textbf{Resource Interpretation} \\
\hline
$A \multimap B$ & Linear function & Consumes one $A$, produces one $B$ \\
$A \otimes B$ & Linear pair & Has one $A$ and one $B$ \\
$A \parr B$ & Linear copair & Can produce $A$ or $B$ \\
$!A$ & Unrestricted value & Can use $A$ any number of times \\
$1$ & Empty resource & No resources \\
$\bot$ & Absurd resource & Impossible state \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{example}[Linear Types for File Handles]\label{ex:linear-files}
A file handle can be given a linear type:
\[\mathrm{open} : \mathrm{String} \multimap \mathrm{Handle}\]
\[\mathrm{read} : \mathrm{Handle} \multimap \mathrm{String} \otimes \mathrm{Handle}\]
\[\mathrm{close} : \mathrm{Handle} \multimap \mathbf{1}\]
The linear type ensures that every opened file is closed exactly once---it cannot be discarded (leaked) or duplicated (double-close).
\end{example}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Checking Resource Use in a Tensor Proof}

In the tensor introduction rule, the contexts of the two premises
must be disjoint. An explicitly supplied proof can be checked without
searching for this split: tag each assumption with an occurrence
identifier and compute which identifiers each subtree uses. The
input context maps distinct identifiers to atomic formulas. Its
proof tree consists only of leaves $\mathrm{use}(i)$ and binary
nodes $\mathrm{pair}(p,q)$. The checker synthesizes a tensor tree
with atomic leaves. To check a claimed sequent, require this tree
to equal the claimed conclusion syntactically and the final set
of used identifiers to equal the entire context domain.

\begin{sudocode}[Synthesize a tensor and its footprint]
\Function{UseSet}{$p,\Gamma$}
  \If{$p=\mathrm{use}(i)$}
    \State Require $i\in\operatorname{dom}(\Gamma)$
    \State \Return $(\Gamma[i],\{i\})$ \Comment{One occurrence}
  \EndIf
  \State Read $p$ as $\mathrm{pair}(q,r)$
  \State $(A,L)\gets\Call{UseSet}{q,\Gamma}$
  \State $(B,R)\gets\Call{UseSet}{r,\Gamma}$
  \If{$L\cap R\ne\varnothing$}
    \State Reject reuse \Comment{Premises cannot share a resource}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $(A\otimes B,L\cup R)$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Take assumptions $4:P$, $7:P$, and $9:Q$.
The inner pair of $\mathrm{use}(7)$ and $\mathrm{use}(9)$
synthesizes $P\otimes Q$ and $\{7,9\}$. Combining it with
$\mathrm{use}(4)$ on the left gives $P\otimes(P\otimes Q)$
and $\{4,7,9\}$. The full footprint matches the context, so
the claimed sequent with that conclusion passes. Replacing
$4$ by $7$ in the left leaf preserves the conclusion formula
but fails at the root because both branches use occurrence $7$.
Omitting the left leaf entirely leaves assumption $4$ unused
and fails the final equality check instead.

\Cref{fig:visual-11} compares two proof trees with identical leaf
formulas but different resource use.

