\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
A successful subtree proves its synthesized formula from exactly
its returned footprint. At a leaf this is the identity rule;
at a pair the disjoint union is precisely the tensor rule's
context split. This gives a soundness argument by induction
on the certificate, not by checking example trees. If a
context contains several copies of a formula, distinct identifiers
are indispensable. The procedure neither searches for proofs
nor handles implication, exponentials, or other rules outside
this fragment.

\section{Polarized Type Theory}\label{sec:ch-polarized}

\begin{definition}[Polarity]\label{def:polarity}
In polarized type theory, types are classified as either \emph{positive} (defined by their constructors) or \emph{negative} (defined by their destructors):
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Positive types}: sum types, natural numbers, lists---defined by how they are built
  \item \emph{Negative types}: function types, product types---defined by how they are used
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
The polarity distinction is fundamental to the design of programming languages. Positive types support pattern matching (case analysis); negative types support projection (field access, function application). The evaluation strategy (call-by-value vs.\ call-by-name) determines which polarity is ``privileged'': call-by-value privileges positive types, call-by-name privileges negative types.
\end{insight}

\section{Exercises}\label{sec:ch-exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Find the program corresponding to the proof of $(A \to B) \to ((B \to C) \to (A \to C))$.
  
  \item Show that the type $(A \to B) \to A$ is not inhabited (it corresponds to Peirce's law, which is not intuitionistically valid).
  
  \item Write a cut-free sequent calculus proof of $\Rightarrow (A \land B) \to (B \land A)$ and translate it to a program.
  
  \item Prove that cut elimination preserves the end-sequent.
  
  \item Show that the program corresponding to a normal proof of $A \lor B$ must be either $\mathrm{inl}(a)$ or $\mathrm{inr}(b)$.
  
  \item Formalize the statement ``every even number greater than 2 is the sum of two primes'' as a type in dependent type theory.
  
  \item Explain why the law of excluded middle $A \lor \neg A$ is not inhabited in the STLC.
  
  \item Show that the Curry--Howard correspondence extends to modal logic with a comonad (the ``necessity'' modality $\Box A$).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Chapter Summary}\label{sec:ch-summary}

The Curry--Howard correspondence reveals the deep unity between logic and computation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Propositional proofs correspond to programs in the simply-typed lambda calculus.
  \item Predicate proofs correspond to programs in dependent type theory.
  \item Cut elimination corresponds to program simplification (beta-reduction).
  \item Normalization of proofs corresponds to evaluation of programs.
  \item Proof irrelevance vs.\ proof relevance distinguishes classical from constructive mathematics.
  \item The correspondence enables verified compilation (CompCert) and translation validation.
\end{itemize}

In the next chapter, we turn to computability and complexity---the fundamental limits of what can be computed and how efficiently.

\section{Cut Elimination in Detail}\label{sec:cut-elimination-detail}

\subsection{The Cut Rule and Its Elimination}

The cut rule in sequent calculus allows us to combine two proofs by using an intermediate formula:

\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma \vdash A \quad \Gamma, A \vdash B}{\Gamma \vdash B}\;\mathrm{cut}$
\end{center}

Under the Curry--Howard correspondence, a cut corresponds to a \emph{let-binding}:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma \vdash a : A \quad \Gamma, x:A \vdash b : B}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{let}\;x = a\;\mathrm{in}\;b : B}$
\end{center}

Cut elimination replaces this with \emph{substitution}: we replace every occurrence of $x$ in $b$ with $a$, yielding $b[a/x]$.

\begin{theorem}[Cut Elimination (Gentzen)]\label{thm:cut-elim}
Every proof in the sequent calculus can be transformed into a cut-free proof of the same end-sequent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof Sketch]
The proof proceeds by induction on the \emph{complexity} of the cut, measured by the pair $(\mathrm{depth}(A), |\Pi_1| + |\Pi_2|)$ where $\Pi_1$ and $\Pi_2$ are the subproofs. The key observation is that the principal formula $A$ of the cut is introduced by some logical rule in $\Pi_1$ (on the right) and consumed by some logical rule in $\Pi_2$ (on the left). We can ``push'' the cut upward past these rules, reducing the complexity.

For example, if $A = B \land C$ was introduced by $\land$-right in $\Pi_1$:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\dfrac{\Pi_1'}{\Gamma \vdash B} \quad \dfrac{\Pi_1''}{\Gamma \vdash C}}{\Gamma \vdash B \land C}\;\land\text{-R} \qquad \dfrac{\Pi_2}{\Gamma, B \land C \vdash D}$
\end{center}
We replace this with two smaller cuts:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\dfrac{\Pi_1'}{\Gamma \vdash B} \quad \dfrac{\dfrac{\Pi_1''}{\Gamma \vdash C} \quad \dfrac{\Pi_2}{\Gamma, B \land C \vdash D}}{\Gamma, B, C \vdash D}\;\land\text{-L}}{\Gamma \vdash D}\;\mathrm{cut}$
\end{center}
The new cuts have strictly smaller complexity, so the process terminates.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Consequences of Cut Elimination}

\begin{theorem}[Subformula Property]\label{thm:subformula}
Every cut-free proof of $\Gamma \vdash C$ contains only formulas that are subformulas of formulas in $\Gamma$ or $C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Consistency]\label{cor:consistency}
The sequent calculus is consistent: $\nvdash \bot$. If $\vdash \bot$ had a proof, by cut elimination it would have a cut-free proof, but $\bot$ has no subformulas, so no cut-free proof can exist.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Decidability of Propositional Logic]\label{cor:decidability}
Intuitionistic propositional logic is decidable. Since cut-free proofs have the subformula property, there are only finitely many possible cut-free proofs of a given sequent. We can enumerate them all.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Cut Elimination as Program Optimization}

Under the Curry--Howard correspondence, cut elimination corresponds to program optimization:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Cut elimination as program transformation}
\label{tab:cut-as-opt}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Proof Transformation} & \textbf{Program Transformation} \\
\hline
Eliminate cut on $\land$ & Inline a pair and its projections \\
Eliminate cut on $\lor$ & Inline a case analysis and its injections \\
Eliminate cut on $\to$ & Beta-reduction: $(\lambda x.\,b)\;a \longrightarrow b[a/x]$ \\
Eliminate cut on $\forall$ & Type application: $(\Lambda \alpha.\,e)\;[\tau] \longrightarrow e[\tau/\alpha]$ \\
Eliminate cut on $\exists$ & Unpack and re-pack an existential \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{example}[Beta-Reduction as Cut Elimination]\label{ex:beta-cut}
Consider the proof of $B$ from the assumption $A$:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\dfrac{}{A \vdash A}\;\mathrm{ax} \quad \dfrac{\dfrac{}{B \vdash B}\;\mathrm{ax}}{A \vdash A \to B}\;\to\text{-R}}{A \vdash B}\;\mathrm{cut}$
\end{center}
This corresponds to the program $\mathrm{let}\;x = \lambda y.\,y\;\mathrm{in}\;x\;a$, which reduces to $a$ by beta-reduction.
\end{example}

\subsection{Normalization and Strong Normalization}

\begin{definition}[Strong Normalization]\label{def:strong-norm}
A term $t$ is \emph{strongly normalizing} (SN) if every reduction sequence starting from $t$ is finite. A type system is strongly normalizing if every well-typed term is SN.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Strong Normalization of STLC]\label{thm:sn-stlc}
Every well-typed term in the simply-typed lambda calculus is strongly normalizing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof Sketch]
We use a \emph{reducibility candidate} argument (Tait's method). For each type $\tau$, we define a set $\mathcal{R}(\tau)$ of ``reducible'' terms of type $\tau$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathcal{R}(\iota) = \{n \mid n \text{ is a normal form of type } \iota\}$
  \item $\mathcal{R}(A \to B) = \{f \mid \forall a \in \mathcal{R}(A).\;f\;a \in \mathcal{R}(B)\}$
\end{itemize}
We then prove by induction on typing derivations that every well-typed term is in the reducibility set of its type. The key lemma is that substitution preserves reducibility.
\end{proof}

\section{Proof Irrelevance and Proof Relevance}\label{sec:proof-irrelevance}

\subsection{The Concept of Proof Irrelevance}

In classical mathematics, we typically care only about \emph{whether} a proposition is true, not \emph{how} it is proved. This principle is called \emph{proof irrelevance}:

\begin{definition}[Proof Irrelevance]\label{def:proof-irrelevance}
A type theory has \emph{proof irrelevance} if for any proposition $P$ and any two proofs $p_1, p_2 : P$, we have $p_1 = p_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Proof Irrelevance in Coq]
In Coq, types in \texttt{Prop} are proof-irrelevant by default (with some subtleties). This means that if $P : \texttt{Prop}$ and $p_1\;p_2 : P$, then $p_1$ and $p_2$ are interchangeable for the purposes of \emph{extraction} to OCaml or Haskell: they are erased during extraction.
\end{example}

\subsection{Proof Relevance in Type Theory}

In contrast, \emph{proof-relevant} type theories treat proofs as first-class objects that can be distinguished:

\begin{example}[Proof Relevance in HoTT]
In Homotopy Type Theory (HoTT), a proof of $a =_A b$ is a \emph{path} from $a$ to $b$ in the space $A$. Different paths can be genuinely different:
\begin{itemize}
  \item In the circle type $S^1$, there are infinitely many distinct paths from $\mathrm{base}$ to $\mathrm{base}$ (winding around the circle different numbers of times).
  \item In a group $G$ viewed as a one-object groupoid, proofs of $g = h$ correspond to elements of $G$.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Proof irrelevance vs.\ proof relevance}
\label{tab:proof-irrel-vs-rel}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Proof Irrelevant} & \textbf{Proof Relevant} \\
\hline
Equality of proofs & All proofs of $P$ are equal & Proofs can be distinguished \\
Erasure & Proofs can be erased & Proofs carry computational content \\
Example systems & Coq's \texttt{Prop}, PVS & HoTT, Cubical Agda, MLTT \\
Mathematical tradition & Classical mathematics & Constructive mathematics \\
Identity type & $\mathrm{Id}_A(a, b)$ is a proposition & $\mathrm{Id}_A(a, b)$ can have multiple elements \\
Univalence & Not compatible & Compatible (Voevodsky) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Propositions as Types vs.\ Propositions as Types in Prop}

Many proof assistants distinguish between two ``sorts'' of types:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Proof-irrelevant propositions} (\texttt{Prop} in Coq, \texttt{Prop} in Lean): Used for logical statements. Proofs are erased during extraction. Proof irrelevance is assumed or enforced.
  \item \textbf{Proof-relevant types} (\texttt{Type} in Coq, \texttt{Type} in Lean): Used for data. Terms carry computational content. Pattern matching and case analysis are computationally meaningful.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}[Large Elimination]
\emph{Large elimination} is the ability to pattern-match on a proof in \texttt{Prop} to produce a value in \texttt{Type}. This is restricted in Coq to maintain proof irrelevance:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Allowed: matching on an \emph{empty} type (to produce anything, since there are no cases).
  \item Allowed: matching on a \emph{singleton} type (since all proofs are equal).
  \item Forbidden: matching on a general proposition to produce data.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{The Axiom of Proof Irrelevance}

In systems that do not enforce proof irrelevance by default, it can be added as an axiom:

\begin{definition}[Proof Irrelevance Axiom]\label{def:proof-irrel-axiom}
The \emph{axiom of proof irrelevance} states:
\[\forall (P : \mathrm{Prop})\;(p_1\;p_2 : P).\;p_1 = p_2\]
This axiom is consistent with the Calculus of Inductive Constructions but is incompatible with the univalence axiom of HoTT.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Proof Irrelevance and Excluded Middle]\label{thm:irrel-em}
Proof irrelevance implies a weak form of excluded middle: for any proposition $P$, either $P$ or $\neg P$ is inhabited. This is because if $P$ is inhabited, all proofs are equal, so we can decide $P$ by checking whether a proof exists.
\end{theorem}

\begin{insight}
The tension between proof irrelevance and proof relevance is one of the deepest questions in the foundations of mathematics. Classical mathematics treats proofs as irrelevant certificates of truth. Constructive mathematics, especially in the HoTT tradition, treats proofs as mathematical objects in their own right---paths, homotopies, and higher-dimensional structures. The choice between these perspectives shapes the entire character of a type theory.
\end{insight}
